                "A solar flare is the most violent energy release in the solar atmosphere. Within a few minutes, particles are accelerated and radiation surges from hard X-rays to microwaves, while the near-Earth environment is disturbed. Yet the field that drives the whole event has kept one of its best secrets: nobody could say what the magnetic field in the lower atmosphere actually does during those minutes.",
                "The omission was not from lack of interest. The standard approach reads polarization signatures in the solar spectrum, then inverts a radiative-transfer model to infer field strength. A flare distorts the very line profiles being measured, so the model's assumptions collapse and the inversion becomes unreliable or simply fails.",
